{"id":1964,"date":"2026-03-05T19:23:56","date_gmt":"2026-03-05T18:23:56","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/visita-guiada-mahon-menorca\/"},"modified":"2026-07-02T13:03:07","modified_gmt":"2026-07-02T11:03:07","slug":"visita-guiada-mahon-menorca","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/visita-guiada-mahon-menorca\/","title":{"rendered":"Discover Ma\u00f3: The Ultimate Guide to Visiting Menorca&#8217;s Capital"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Ma\u00f3 (Mah\u00f3n in Spanish) is the capital of Menorca and a city that uniquely blends history, sea and Mediterranean life. Its impressive <strong>natural harbour<\/strong>\u2014one of the largest in the Mediterranean\u2014has been a strategic hub since Antiquity, attracting Talayotic settlements, Roman merchants, British strategists and travellers from all over the world. This guide is written by <strong>Daniel Vaquera<\/strong>, an official local guide, and shows you the city just as we do on our <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/field-trips\/\">Mah\u00f3n guided tour<\/a>.  <\/p>\n<h2>In this guide<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#historia\">\ud83c\udffa A brief history of Ma\u00f3: from Talayotic port to capital<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#puerto\">\u2693 The harbour: 5 kilometres of history<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#casco\">\ud83e\udded The old town: squares, markets and viewpoints<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#monumentos\">\ud83c\udfdb\ufe0f Must-see landmarks<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#museos\">\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Museums and culture<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#comer\">\ud83c\udf77 Where to eat and what to order<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#curiosidades\">\ud83d\udca1 Curiosities almost nobody tells you<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#alrededores\">\ud83c\udfd6\ufe0f The surrounding area<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#clima\">\ud83c\udf24\ufe0f Climate: when to come<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#practico\">\u2139\ufe0f Practical information<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"#faq\">\u2753 Frequently Asked Questions<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"historia\">\ud83c\udffa A brief history of Ma\u00f3: from Talayotic port to capital<\/h2>\n<h3>The origins: Talayotics, Carthaginians and Romans<\/h3>\n<p>The area around the harbour has been inhabited for thousands of years: on the outskirts of the city, the Talayotic settlement of <strong>Trepuc\u00f3<\/strong>\u2014with one of the largest taulas on the island\u2014reminds us that people lived here long before Ma\u00f3 existed. Tradition attributes the city\u2019s name to the Carthaginian general <strong>Mago<\/strong>, Hannibal\u2019s brother, and with the Roman conquest in <strong>123 BC<\/strong> the site was consolidated as <strong>Portus Magonis<\/strong>. <\/p>\n<h3>The British century: the capital that arrived by sea<\/h3>\n<p>The big turning point came in the <strong>18th century<\/strong>. The British, who ruled Menorca in three periods between <strong>1708 and 1802<\/strong>, quickly understood the harbour\u2019s military value, and in <strong>1722<\/strong> Governor <strong>Richard Kane<\/strong> moved the capital from Ciutadella to Ma\u00f3. From that era we still have the <strong>Cam\u00ed d&#8217;en Kane<\/strong>, sash windows and <em>boinders<\/em> (bow windows), a taste for gin\u2026 and a good share of the anglicisms in Menorcan Catalan.  <\/p>\n<h3>Bourgeois and seafaring Ma\u00f3<\/h3>\n<p>During the 19th century, Ma\u00f3 enjoyed its commercial golden age: the fortress of <strong>La Mola<\/strong> (1852) protected the harbour entrance, the <strong>Teatre Principal<\/strong> premiered Italian operas, and merchants built the stately homes that still give the centre its character. Today it\u2019s a calm, authentic city, where life still revolves around the market, the squares and the harbour. <\/p>\n<h2 id=\"puerto\">\u2693 The harbour: 5 kilometres of history<\/h2>\n<p>Ma\u00f3 harbour is the city\u2019s reason for being: an estuary almost <strong>5 kilometres<\/strong> long, deep and sheltered, considered one of the <strong>largest natural harbours in the Mediterranean<\/strong>. Walking along the Moll de Llevant promenade at sunset, with terraces lit up and sailboats coming in, is one of the city\u2019s great pleasures. <\/p>\n<p>Inside the harbour there\u2019s a small archipelago steeped in history:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Illa del Rei:<\/strong> where <strong>Alfonso III landed in 1287<\/strong> for the Christian conquest. The British built their <strong>naval hospital<\/strong> here in 1711; today, restored, it also houses an international art gallery. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Illa del Llatzeret:<\/strong> the great late-18th-century quarantine fortress, where crews were quarantined. It can be visited in season. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Illa Pinto and Illa de la Quarantena:<\/strong> former naval bases that complete the ensemble.<\/li>\n<li><strong>La Mola (Fortalesa d&#8217;Isabel II):<\/strong> at the northern harbour entrance, an impressive 19th-century defensive complex open to visitors all year round.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Guide tip:<\/strong> a <strong>glass-bottom boat<\/strong> trip around the harbour is the best way to understand Ma\u00f3: from the water you can read three centuries of military and health history in a single ride.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"casco\">\ud83e\udded The old town: squares, markets and viewpoints<\/h2>\n<h3>Pla\u00e7a de la Constituci\u00f3<\/h3>\n<p>Ma\u00f3\u2019s civic heart, dominated by the <strong>Town Hall<\/strong>\u2014with its clock brought by Richard Kane\u2014and the church of <strong>Santa Maria<\/strong>. Its layout reflects the 18th-century urban reorganisation. <\/p>\n<h3>Pla\u00e7a d&#8217;Espanya, Mercat des Peix and the Costa de ses Voltes<\/h3>\n<p>The <strong>fish market<\/strong> (1927) is now also a spot for tapas and vermouth, and the staircase of the <strong>Costa de ses Voltes<\/strong> connects the upper town with the harbour in one of Ma\u00f3\u2019s most photographed scenes.<\/p>\n<h3>Es Claustre del Carme<\/h3>\n<p>The former neoclassical cloister of the Carme convent houses the <strong>municipal market<\/strong>: Ma\u00f3-Menorca cheese, sobrasada, rustic bread and fresh produce under historic arcades. A must-stop to understand the island\u2019s pantry. <\/p>\n<h3>Viewpoints<\/h3>\n<p>From the <strong>Pont des Castell<\/strong>, the viewpoint at Pla\u00e7a de la Miranda or the des Freginal park, you\u2019ll get the best panoramas of the harbour and its islands.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"monumentos\">\ud83c\udfdb\ufe0f Must-see landmarks<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Church of Santa Maria.<\/strong> Rebuilt in the 18th century, it holds one of the city\u2019s treasures: the <strong>monumental organ<\/strong> with more than 3,000 pipes, built by Swiss master Johann Kyburz and installed in <strong>1810<\/strong>. From May to October, recitals are held almost daily. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Teatre Principal (1829).<\/strong> The <strong>oldest operating opera house in Spain<\/strong>, with its elegant horseshoe-shaped auditorium designed for acoustics. It still programmes opera, music and theatre all year round. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Birthplace of Mateu Orfila.<\/strong> The Ma\u00f3-born <strong>Mateu Orfila (1787\u20131853)<\/strong> is considered the <strong>father of modern toxicology<\/strong>; a bust commemorates his legacy.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sant Francesc.<\/strong>  The convent complex that closes off the city to the north, with its Baroque fa\u00e7ade and the cloister that now houses the Museu de Menorca.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Arco de Sant Roc.<\/strong>  The only remaining vestige of Ma\u00f3\u2019s medieval wall.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"museos\">\ud83d\uddbc\ufe0f Museums and culture<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Museu de Menorca<\/strong> (Sant Francesc cloister): the island\u2019s main museum, with collections ranging from the Talayotic world to British Menorca. A must if you\u2019re interested in <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/menorca-talayotica-guia\/\">Talayotic Menorca<\/a>. <\/li>\n<li><strong>Ca n&#8217;Oliver:<\/strong> a bourgeois mansion with the Hern\u00e1ndez Sanz\u2013Hern\u00e1ndez Mora collection and magnificent views from its tower.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Museu Hern\u00e1ndez Sanz and the rooms of Es Claustre del Carme:<\/strong> exhibitions about Ma\u00f3 in the 18th and 19th centuries.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"comer\">\ud83c\udf77 Where to eat and what to order in Ma\u00f3<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Moll de Llevant:<\/strong> the line of harbourfront restaurants, ideal for fresh fish, rice dishes and seafood stews with views of the boats. <a href=\"https:\/\/www.google.com\/maps\/search\/?api=1&#038;query=restaurantes+Moll+de+Llevant+Ma%C3%B3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">View on Google Maps<\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Mercat des Peix:<\/strong> seafood tapas and vermouth at the fish market. <a href=\"https:\/\/www.google.com\/maps\/search\/?api=1&#038;query=Mercat+des+Peix+Ma%C3%B3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">View on Google Maps<\/a><\/li>\n<li><strong>Es Claustre del Carme:<\/strong> local produce to take away or snack on under the arcades. <a href=\"https:\/\/www.google.com\/maps\/search\/?api=1&#038;query=Claustre+del+Carme+Ma%C3%B3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">View on Google Maps<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>What to order: flavours of Ma\u00f3<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Seafood stew and sailors\u2019 rice dishes<\/strong>, with the harbour right in front of you.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ma\u00f3-Menorca D.O.P. cheese<\/strong>, which takes the city\u2019s name from the harbour it was exported from.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mahonesa sauce:<\/strong> yes, mayonnaise was born here (see curiosities).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Xoriguer gin and pomada:<\/strong> the distillery, a British legacy, is still right on the harbour and can be visited.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ensa\u00efmada, pastissets and cuscuss\u00f3<\/strong> for something sweet.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"curiosidades\">\ud83d\udca1 Curiosities almost nobody tells you<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mayonnaise is from Ma\u00f3.<\/strong>  According to tradition, after the French capture in 1756, the Duke of Richelieu\u2019s cook took to Paris the \u201cmahonesa sauce\u201d he had discovered on the island.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The father of toxicology was from Ma\u00f3.<\/strong>  Mateu Orfila became dean of the Faculty of Medicine in Paris.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Spain\u2019s oldest operating opera house isn\u2019t in Madrid<\/strong> or Barcelona: it\u2019s the Teatre Principal in Ma\u00f3 (1829).<\/li>\n<li><strong>The Town Hall clock was brought by a British governor<\/strong>, Richard Kane\u2014the same man who moved the capital and built the road that bears his name.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The \u201cboinders\u201d<\/strong>\u2014wooden bay windows\u2014are Menorca\u2019s version of English bow windows.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The harbour once hosted the Royal Navy\u2019s Mediterranean base<\/strong>, and legend places Admiral Nelson at Golden Farm, the red estate overlooking the north shore.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"alrededores\">\ud83c\udfd6\ufe0f The surrounding area of Ma\u00f3<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Es Castell:<\/strong> the most British town on the island (the former Georgetown), with the charming Cales Fonts.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Trepuc\u00f3 and Talat\u00ed de Dalt:<\/strong> two Talayotic settlements just outside the city.<\/li>\n<li><strong>S&#8217;Albufera des Grau Natural Park:<\/strong> the heart of the <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/menorca-biosphere-reserve\/\">Biosphere Reserve<\/a>, 15 minutes away.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Fav\u00e0ritx Lighthouse:<\/strong> Menorca\u2019s most photographed lunar landscape (more in our <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/guia-faros-menorca\/\">lighthouse guide<\/a>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Binib\u00e8quer Vell and the southern beaches of Sant Llu\u00eds:<\/strong> 10\u201315 minutes by car.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"clima\">\ud83c\udf24\ufe0f Climate and average temperature: when to come<\/h2>\n<p>Ma\u00f3 shares the island\u2019s Mediterranean climate, with the tramontana a regular winter visitor. Facing east, its <strong>sunrises over the harbour<\/strong> are as memorable as Ciutadella\u2019s sunsets. <\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Month<\/th>\n<th>Avg temp<\/th>\n<th>Sea<\/th>\n<th>Vibe<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>January<\/td>\n<td>~11\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~14\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>Quiet, ideal for museums and the harbour<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>April<\/td>\n<td>~14\u201315\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~15\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>Perfect for strolling the old town<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>May<\/td>\n<td>~17\u201318\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~17\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>Excellent: terraces and harbour cruises<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>June<\/td>\n<td>~21\u201322\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~22\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>Summer; organ concerts begin<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>July<\/td>\n<td>~24\u201325\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~25\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>Peak summer; Festes de la Mare de D\u00e9u de Gr\u00e0cia coming up (7\u20139 Sept)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>August<\/td>\n<td>~25\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~26\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>The warmest and liveliest month<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>September<\/td>\n<td>~22\u201323\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~25\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>Unbeatable: Gr\u00e0cia festivities and warm sea<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>December<\/td>\n<td>~12\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>~16\u00b0C<\/td>\n<td>Mild; Christmas lights and atmosphere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>When to come?<\/strong> For good weather without the crowds, <strong>June and September<\/strong> (with the <strong>Festes de Gr\u00e0cia<\/strong>, from 7 to 9 September, as the finishing touch). For culture and walking, <strong>April, May and October<\/strong>. <\/p>\n<h2 id=\"practico\">\u2139\ufe0f Practical information for visiting Ma\u00f3<\/h2>\n<h3>How to get there and get around<\/h3>\n<p>Menorca <strong>Airport is just 10 minutes<\/strong> from the centre. Ma\u00f3 is also the island\u2019s main ferry port. The old town is easy to explore <strong>on foot<\/strong>; for the harbour, bear in mind the hill (or the ses Voltes staircase).  <\/p>\n<h3>Where to park<\/h3>\n<p>Car parks by the harbour (Moll de Llevant) and in the s&#8217;Esplanada area; paid street parking in the centre. In summer, earlier is better. <\/p>\n<h3>The best way to discover Ma\u00f3: with a local guide<\/h3>\n<p>Ma\u00f3 makes much more sense when someone connects the dots: why the capital changed cities, what the Royal Navy was doing in the harbour, or where mayonnaise was born. On our <a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/field-trips\/\"><strong>Mah\u00f3n guided tour<\/strong><\/a> we walk through the old town and the harbour viewpoints in around <strong>two hours<\/strong>, in Spanish, Catalan, English or French. <\/p>\n<p><a href=\"https:\/\/wa.me\/34649902301?text=Hola%2C%20me%20gustar%C3%ADa%20reservar%20una%20visita%20guiada%20por%20Mah%C3%B3n.\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>Book your guided tour on WhatsApp<\/strong><\/a> and we\u2019ll get back to you as soon as possible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"faq\">\u2753 Frequently Asked Questions<\/h2>\n<h3>How much time do I need to see Ma\u00f3?<\/h3>\n<p>For the historic centre, museums, the market and a walk around the harbour, ideally you\u2019ll want <strong>a full day<\/strong>. With half a day you can see the main highlights. <\/p>\n<h3>Are there concerts at Santa Maria?<\/h3>\n<p>Yes: the <strong>historic organ<\/strong> offers regular recitals, especially between <strong>May and October<\/strong>. Check the times at the church. <\/p>\n<h3>Can you visit the harbour islands?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Illa del Rei<\/strong> and <strong>Llatzeret<\/strong> can be visited in season with boat departures from the harbour; <strong>La Mola<\/strong> is open all year round.<\/p>\n<h3>Ma\u00f3 or Mah\u00f3n?<\/h3>\n<p>Both: <strong>Ma\u00f3<\/strong> is the Catalan\/Menorcan name and <strong>Mah\u00f3n<\/strong> is Spanish. The official name is Ma\u00f3-Mah\u00f3n. <\/p>\n<h2>More Menorca guides<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/what-to-see-in-ciutadella-menorca\/\">What to see in Ciutadella de Menorca \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/mahon-in-winter\/\">Mah\u00f3n in winter: routes and plans \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/menorca-talayotica-guia\/\">Talayotic Menorca \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/menorca-biosphere-reserve\/\">Menorca, Biosphere Reserve \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/en\/guia-faros-menorca\/\">Menorca\u2019s 7 lighthouses \u2192<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><script type=\"application\/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https:\/\/schema.org\",\n  \"@graph\": [\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"BlogPosting\",\n      \"mainEntityOfPage\": {\n        \"@type\": \"WebPage\",\n        \"@id\": \"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/visita-guiada-mahon-menorca\/\"\n      },\n      \"headline\": \"Descubre Ma\u00f3: La gu\u00eda definitiva para visitar la capital de Menorca\",\n      \"description\": \"Gu\u00eda local de Ma\u00f3 (Mah\u00f3n): historia, el gran puerto natural, Teatre Principal, Santa Maria y su \u00f3rgano, mercados, d\u00f3nde comer, curiosidades y clima. Por un gu\u00eda oficial de Voyager Menorca.\",\n      \"author\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Person\",\n        \"name\": \"Daniel Vaquera\",\n        \"jobTitle\": \"Gu\u00eda oficial de turismo de Menorca\",\n        \"worksFor\": {\n          \"@type\": \"Organization\",\n          \"name\": \"Voyager Menorca\",\n          \"url\": \"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"publisher\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Organization\",\n        \"name\": \"Voyager Menorca\",\n        \"logo\": {\n          \"@type\": \"ImageObject\",\n          \"url\": \"https:\/\/voyagermenorca.com\/logo.png\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"datePublished\": \"2026-03-05\",\n      \"dateModified\": \"2026-07-02\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n      \"mainEntity\": [\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"\u00bfCu\u00e1nto tiempo necesito para ver Ma\u00f3?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"Para el centro hist\u00f3rico, los museos, el mercado y un paseo por el puerto, lo ideal es un d\u00eda completo. Con medio d\u00eda puedes ver lo m\u00e1s emblem\u00e1tico.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"\u00bfHay conciertos en la iglesia de Santa Maria?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"S\u00ed, el \u00f3rgano hist\u00f3rico de Santa Maria ofrece recitales regulares, especialmente entre mayo y octubre.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"\u00bfSe pueden visitar las islas del puerto de Ma\u00f3?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"La Illa del Rei y el Llatzeret se visitan en temporada con salidas en barco desde el puerto; la fortaleza de La Mola abre todo el a\u00f1o.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"\u00bfSe dice Ma\u00f3 o Mah\u00f3n?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"Ambas son correctas: Ma\u00f3 es el nombre en catal\u00e1n\/menorqu\u00edn y Mah\u00f3n en castellano. La denominaci\u00f3n oficial es Ma\u00f3-Mah\u00f3n.\"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"TouristTrip\",\n      \"name\": \"Visita guiada de Mah\u00f3n\",\n      \"description\": \"Tour a pie por el casco antiguo y los miradores del puerto de Ma\u00f3 con un gu\u00eda local oficial.\",\n      \"provider\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Organization\",\n        \"name\": \"Voyager Menorca\"\n      },\n      \"itinerary\": [\n        { \"@type\": \"TouristAttraction\", \"name\": \"Pla\u00e7a de la Constituci\u00f3\" },\n        { \"@type\": \"TouristAttraction\", \"name\": \"Iglesia de Santa Maria\" },\n        { \"@type\": \"TouristAttraction\", \"name\": \"Teatre Principal\" },\n        { \"@type\": \"TouristAttraction\", \"name\": \"Mercat des Peix\" },\n        { \"@type\": \"TouristAttraction\", \"name\": \"Puerto de Ma\u00f3\" }\n      ],\n      \"offers\": {\n        \"@type\": \"Offer\",\n        \"priceCurrency\": \"EUR\",\n        \"url\": \"https:\/\/wa.me\/34649902301\",\n        \"availability\": \"https:\/\/schema.org\/InStock\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n<\/script><\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Discover Ma\u00f3: history, culture and unforgettable corners. Ma\u00f3 (Mah\u00f3n in Spanish) is the capital of Menorca and a city that combines history, sea and Mediterranean life in a unique way. Its impressive natural harbor has been a strategic hub since ancient times, attracting early Talayotic settlements, Roman merchants and British strategists.  <\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":2,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"footnotes":""},"categories":[72,74,243,117],"tags":[297,244,298,87],"class_list":["post-1964","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-discover-menorca","category-family","category-guided-tour-mahon","category-guides-and-routes","tag-guided-tour-mahon","tag-mahon","tag-mao","tag-menorca"],"yoast_head":"<!-- This site is optimized with the Yoast SEO Premium plugin v28.3 (Yoast SEO v28.8) - https:\/\/yoast.com\/product\/yoast-seo-premium-wordpress\/ -->\n<title>What to see in Mah\u00f3n (Ma\u00f3): A guide to the capital of Menorca<\/title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Discover the beauty of Mah\u00f3n with a guided tour. Learn about the history and culture of this wonderful city.\" \/>\n<meta name=\"ro